+### RL-124 — Minimal-top latency sweeps are serialized-word controls, not line-DRAM projections
+
+- **Status:** confirmed source and waveform audits; matched complete capture controls at both minimal-top settings and full-burst latency 40
+- **Scope:** `cosim/black-parrot-minimal-example`, two resident/four logical contexts, one fixed ELF, 32-line side store, pipelined responder latency 40/200 and queue four
+- **Claim:** minimal capture on/off gives identical cold/target-warm/source-warm/switch-only windows of 5,544/807/1,474/721 at latency 40 and 26,024/3,367/6,594/3,281 at 200. Its cold window contains 128 serialized 32-bit ARLEN-zero reads; every first response is exactly 40 or 200 ticks after AR. The 20,480-cycle increase equals 160 times 128, not a whole-line DRAM projection. Full-burst capture on/off instead gives identical 664/197/254/111 windows at latency 40: eight 64-byte cold bursts, first response at 40 and last accepted beat at 47, no backpressure. Both transports prove capture engagement but zero side-store-only fetches in every mode; all seven target prefix lines are complete at warm entry and all fifty timed prefix fetches hit ordinary L1.
+- **Evidence:** `research/resume-retention-matrix-20261120/on40/` and `off40/`; `research/demand-capture-fullcore-20261120/`; wrapper assignments in `cosim/black-parrot-minimal-example/v/top_zynq.sv` and burst configuration in `import/black-parrot-subsystems/blackparrot/v/bp_axi_top.sv`.
+- **Meaning:** matching physical side-store bytes does not guarantee extra unique lines beyond L1. Use actual burst transport with independently fixed response bandwidth before estimating realistic latency sensitivity; keep minimal-top runs as conditional functional/capacity diagnostics.
+- **Limits:** these are synthetic nonresident prefix roundtrips with equal prior warmup, not whole Linux applications or FPGA measurements. The all-demand admission policy remains different from selected-prefix retention.
+
+### RL-125 — Natural post-resume demand separates small gains from unresolved endpoints
+
+- **Status:** four checked FPGA diagnostic boots, one independent boot per workload; replication and retention-versus-refresh controls pending
+- **Scope:** original accepted 32-line banked instruction image, four resident/four logical contexts, Linux early userspace-futex hook, private 32 KiB data allocated after fork, 64 measured roundtrips, 32 warmup and four alternating pairs per boot
+- **Claim:** paired complete-roundtrip saved percentages are scan 1.7303% [0.0712,3.6418], lookup 0.2219% [-2.6029,2.3452], parse 1.3474% [0.9556,1.9057], and dependent chase -1.0241% [-5.1875,2.9253]. Lookup changes keys, parsing traverses variable records, and chase advances a shuffled dependent ring; all totals are independently checked outside the steady timed window, behind a terminal drain handshake. No dropped replay triggers or incomplete terminal runs are accepted.
+- **Evidence:** `/home/coyang/fpga-artifacts/context-resume/natural-demand-20261120/session1/` and `session2/`, hash-bound analysis/status/transcripts; `linux-tests/resume_workload.h` and host protocol checks. Each entire physical session holds the common exclusive reservation plus the serialized board-side program lock.
+- **Meaning:** natural data demand does not turn local cache work into uniform complete execution gains. Parsing is a useful replication candidate; lookup and chase remain negative or unresolved controls. Static address-table tuning is unjustified by this evidence.
+- **Limits:** these original-ELF measurements include triggers and interference in each two-switch request, but exclude allocation, fork, warmup, checker and teardown. They are not whole-program or replicated application speedups. A separate optional ELF adds whole-cell timing and actual retention-use telemetry without changing the original ELF bytes.
+
+### RL-126 — First completed demand-line retention is an unaccepted capacity experiment
+
+- **Status:** frozen RTL `2958cd72137d195537b2c86ad11537ea9cd6388e`; directed cache, real CSR decoder/wrapper, full-core context/privilege and default-policy smoke gates pass; route and Linux execution pending
+- **Hypothesis:** ordinary refill round-robin copying duplicates L1 contents in RL-124. Pinning the first complete demand lines after invalidation may preserve earlier useful lines through later instruction pressure without adding speculative reads or another address table. The existing 32-line banked data storage is reused; no new SRAM read ownership or full-line staging is introduced.
+- **Control:** privileged global CSR 0x5c1 selects reset-default legacy mode 0, demand round robin 1, or first completed lines per bank 2. Ordinary fills copy only accepted beats; aborted/partial entries cannot consume the completed-line budget; existing invalidation clears stored validity. Status exposes capability, mode, sticky completion and saturating consumed side-only fetch-response count. The count is neither retired instructions nor a steady-window-only counter.
+- **Evidence:** separate `/home/coyang/bp-linux-retention-policy` branch and `research/retention-policy-directed-extended-20261120/`; S/M access and U read/write denial, global state across logical contexts 1/3, stalls, cancellation, partial admission, fence and saturation gates. Kernel patch 0027 probes through exception-table fixup and returns ENODEV on unsupported hardware; host tests do not establish that physical trap behavior.
+- **Acceptance:** require same-CFG routed fit, exact-image CSR collision rejection, safe old-hardware kernel probe, then matched default/round-robin/first-admission workload results including whole-cell setup, trigger costs, interference and useful side-only consumption. No FPGA performance improvement is claimed.
+
